Pop-sortable elements in Sn under the\nL25@P0: weak order. Claesson and Guðmundsson [7] proved that for each fixed nonnegative integer\nL26@P0: t, the generating function that counts t-Pop-sortable elements in Sn is rational. Defant [9]\nL27@P0: established the analogous rationality result for the generating functions of t-Pop-sortable\nL28@P0: elements of type B and type Ae weak orders.\nL29@P0: Introduced in 1962, the n-th Tamari lattice Tamn consists of semilength-n Dyck paths\nL30@P0: (lattice paths from (0, 0) to (n, n) above the diagonal y = x) [23]; its partial order will\nL31@P0: be defined in Section 2. There are generalizations of the definition, most notably the\nL32@P0: m-Tamari lattices by Bergeron and Pr´eville-Ratelle [4] and the ν-Tamari lattices introduced\nL33@P0: Date: December 2021.\nL34@P0: 1\nL35@P0-1: arXiv:2201.10030v1 [math.CO] 25 Jan 20222 LETONG HONG\nL36@P1: by Pr´eville-Ratelle and Viennot [19]. Fundamental in algebraic combinatorics [17], the n-th\nL37@P1: Tamari lattice Tamn is also isomorphic to Avn(312), the lattice of 312-pattern-avoiding\nL38@P1: permutations under the weak order of Sn [5].\nL39@P1: In this paper, we study the Pop operator on Tamari lattices. Let ht(n) be the number of\nL40@P1: t-Pop-sortable elements in Tamn. A part of a conjecture by Defant [8] is that for every\nL41@P1: fixed t, the generating function P\nL42@P1: n≥1\nL43@P1: ht(n)z\nL44@P1: n\nL45@P1: is rational. We confirm this statement by\nL46@P1: giving the exact formula of the generating function:\nL47@P1: Theorem 1.1. Let ht(n) denote the number of t-Pop-sortable Dyck paths in the n-th\nL48@P1: Tamari lattice Tamn. Then\nL49@P1: X\nL50@P1: n≥1\nL51@P1: ht(n)z\nL52@P1: n =\nL53@P1: z\nL54@P1: 1 − 2z −\nL55@P1: Pt\nL56@P1: j=2 Cj−1z\nL57@P1: j\nL58@P1: ,\nL59@P1: where Cj are the Catalan numbers.\nL60@P1: Moreover, settling a conjecture in Defant and Williams’s paper (Conjecture 11.2 (2) in\nL61@P1: [11]), we have the following theorem:\nL62@P1: Theorem 1.2. Define Pop(L; q) = P\nL63@P1: b∈PopL(L)\nL64@P1: q\nL65@P1: |UL(b)|\nL66@P1: , where UL(b) is the set of elements\nL67@P1: of L that cover b. Then we have\nL68@P1: Pop(Tamn+1; q) = Xn\nL69@P1: k=0\nL70@P1: 1\nL71@P1: k + 1\u0012\nL72@P1: 2k\nL73@P1: k\nL74@P1: \u0013\u0012 n\nL75@P1: 2k\nL76@P1: \u0013\nL77@P1: q\nL78@P1: n−k\nL79@P1: ,\nL80@P1: where the coefficients form OEIS sequence [22] A055151.\nL81@P1: In particular, when q = 1, we have that\nL82@P1: |PopTamn(Tamn)| = Mn−1,\nL83@P1: where Mn is the n-th Motzkin number (OEIS sequence [22] A001006).\nL84@P1: Additional motivation for studying the size of the image of PopTamncomes from a theorem\nL85@P1: by Defant and Williams (Theorem 9.13 in [11]). In that theorem, they proved that\nL86@P1: |Xn| = {y ∈ Tamn | Row(y) ≤ y}, where Row is the rowmotion operator on Tamn (which\nL87@P1: is equivalent to the Kreweras complement operator on noncrossing partitions [10]). They\nL88@P1: also showed that |Xn| is the number of independent dominating sets in a certain graph\nL89@P1: associated with Tamn called its Galois graph.\nL90@P1: The paper is organized as follows. In Section 2 we give the necessary definitions. In Section\nL91@P1-2: 3 and Section 4 we prove Theorem 1.1 and Theorem 1.2.THE POP-STACK-SORTING OPERATOR ON TAMARI LATTICES 3\nL92@P2: Acknowledgements\nL93@P2: The research was conducted at the 2021 University of Minnesota Duluth REU (NSF–DMS\nL94@P2: Grant 1949884 and NSA Grant H98230-20-1-0009) and fully supported by the generosity\nL95@P2: of the CYAN Mathematics Undergraduate Activities Fund. The author is deeply thankful\nL96@P2: to Professor Joseph Gallian for his long-lasting efforts and care in running the wonderful\nL97@P2: program and to Colin Defant for proposing the project and his dedicated mentorship. The\nL98@P2: author is also grateful to Qiuyu Ren and Daniel Zhu for discussions/editing comments\nL99@P2: and to Kenny Lau and Alec Sun for programming assistance.\nL100@P2: 2. Definitions\nL101@P2: 2.1. Lattice basics and the Pop operator.\nL102@P2: Definition 2.1. A meet-semilattice is a poset M such that any two elements x, y ∈ M\nL103@P2: have a greatest lower bound (which is called their meet, denoted by x ∧ y). A lattice L is a\nL104@P2: meet-semilattice such that any two elements x, y ∈ L also have a least upper bound (which\nL105@P2: is called their join, denoted by x ∨ y). A meet-semilattice is complete if every nonempty\nL106@P2: subset A ⊂ M has a meet.\nL107@P2: Given x, y ∈ M, we say that y is covered by x (denoted y l x) if y < x and no z ∈ M\nL108@P2: satisfies y < z < x.\nL109@P2: In this paper we only consider finite meet-semilattices, each of which has a unique minimal\nL110@P2: element ˆ0. They are automatically complete.\nL111@P2: Definition 2.2 ([8]). Let M be a complete meet-semilattice. Define the semilattice\nL112@P2: pop-stack-sorting operator PopM : M → M by\nL113@P2: PopM(x) = ^({y ∈ M : y l x} ∪ {x}).\nL114@P2: Definition 2.3. We say an element x of a complete meet-semilattice M is t-Pop-sortable\nL115@P2: if Popt(x) = ˆ0.\nL116@P2: 2.2. Generalized Tamari lattices. In this paper, a lattice path is a finite planar path\nL117@P2: that starts from the origin and at each step travels either up/N : (0, 1) or right/E : (1, 0).\nL118@P2: Definition 2.4. The horizontal distance of a point p with respect to a lattice path ν is\nL119@P2: the maximum number of east steps one can take starting from p before being strictly to\nL120@P2: the right of ν.\nL121@P2-3: Definition 2.5 ([19]). Let ν be a lattice path from (0, 0) to (` − n, n). The generalized ν\u0002Tamari lattice Tam(ν) is defined as follows:4 LETONG HONG\nL122@P3: (1) elements of Tam(ν) are lattice paths µ from (0, 0) to (` − n, n) that are weakly\nL123@P3: above ν;\nL124@P3: (2) the partial order of Tam(ν) is given by the covering relation: µlµ\nL125@P3: 0\nL126@P3: if µ\nL127@P3: 0\nL128@P3: is obtained\nL129@P3: by shifting a subpath D of µ by 1 unit to the left, where D satisfies (i) it is preceded\nL130@P3: by E; (ii) its first step is N; (iii) its endpoints p, p0 are of the same horizontal distance\nL131@P3: to ν and there is no point between them with the same horizontal distance to ν as\nL132@P3: p. In other words, µ l µ\nL133@P3: 0\nL134@P3: if for such subpath D, µ = XEDY and µ\nL135@P3: 0 = XDEY .\nL136@P3: [8]\nL137@P3: Figure 1. Lattice path µ = NENENEEENE is in Tam(ν) where ν =\nL138@P3: ENNEEEENNE. Each point on µ is labeled with its horizontal distance.\nL139@P3: Definition 2.6. When ν = (NE)\nL140@P3: n\nL141@P3: , the lattice Tam(ν) is the n-th Tamari lattice Tamn\nL142@P3: consisting of the Dyck paths. It is well-known that |Tamn| is the n-th Catalan number Cn.\nL143@P3: 3. Proof of Theorem 1.1\nL144@P3: 3.1. Preliminaries: the ν-bracket vector.\nL145@P3: Definition 3.1. Let b(ν) = (b0(ν), b1(ν), . . . , b`(ν)) be the vector denoting the heights at\nL146@P3: each step of the lattice path ν. Let the fixed position fk denote the largest index such\nL147@P3: that bfk(ν) = k. We say that an integer vector ~b = (b0, b1, . . . , b`) is a ν-bracket vector,\nL148@P3: denoted as ~b ∈ Vec(ν), if\nL149@P3: (1) bfk = k for all k = 0, . . . , n.\nL150@P3: (2) bi(ν) ≤ bi ≤ n for all 0 ≤ i ≤ `.\nL151@P3: (3) If bi = k, then bj ≤ k for all i + 1 ≤ j ≤ fk.\nL152@P3: The partial order of Vec(ν) is defined as follows: we say (b0, b1, . . . , b`) ≤ (b\nL153@P3: 0\nL154@P3: 0\nL155@P3: , b\nL156@P3: 0\nL157@P3: 1\nL158@P3: , . . . , b\nL159@P3: 0\nL160@P3: `\nL161@P3: ) if\nL162@P3: bi ≤ b\nL163@P3: 0\nL164@P3: i\nL165@P3: for all i.\nL166@P3: Remark. An equivalent interpretation of (3) is that ~b is 121-pattern-avoiding. These\nL167@P3: conditions also imply the sequence {bi}\nL168@P3: fk\nL169@P3: fk−1+1 is non-increasing for all k = 0, . . . , n.\nL170@P3: Definition 3.2. Let µ ∈ Tam(ν) be a path from (0, 0) to (` − n, n). We define b(µ) =\nL171@P3: (b0(µ), b1(µ), . . . , b`(µ)) its associated vector as follows: make (` + 1) empty slots;