L207@P4: from ν by deleting its first f0 + 1 steps. Let b\nL208@P4: # be the vector obtained from b by deleting\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Pop-Stack Operator on Ornamentation Lattices (https://arxiv.org/html/2501.10311v1)\nciteturn9517view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2501.10311v1\",\"lineno\":30}); Total lines: 456\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract. L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:     1. cite7†1.1 Ornamentation Lattices L18:     2. cite8†1.2 Pop-Stack Operators L19:     3. cite9†1.3 Main Results L20:     4. cite10†1.4 Outline L21:   3. cite11†2 Basics L22:   4. cite12†3 Maximum Forward Orbit Sizes L23:     1. cite13†3.1 The Lower Bound L24:     2. cite14†3.2 Upper bound L25:   5. cite15†4 The Image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ L26:   6. cite16†5 The Image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$ L27:   7. cite17†6 Semidistributivity L28:   8. cite18†References L29: \nL30: cite19†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L31: arXiv:2501.10311v1 [math.CO] 17 Jan 2025\nL32: # The Pop-Stack Operator on Ornamentation Lattices\nL33: \nL34: Khalid Ajran Address: Department of Mathematics, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA Email address: kajran@mit.edu and Colin Defant Address: Department of Mathematics, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA Email address: colindefant@gmail.com\nL35: ###### Abstract.\nL36: Each rooted plane tree $\\mathsf{T}$ has an associated ornamentation lattice $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$. The ornamentation lattice of an $n$-element chain is the $n$-th Tamari lattice. We study the pop-stack operator $\\mathsf{Pop}\\colon\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})\\to\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$, which sends each element $\\delta$ to the meet of the elements covered by or equal to $\\delta$.\nL37: We compute the maximum size of a forward orbit of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ on $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$, generalizing a result of Defant for Tamari lattices. We also characterize the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ on $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$, generalizing a result of Hong for Tamari lattices. For each integer $k\\geq 0$, we provide necessary conditions for an element of $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$ to be in the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$.\nL38: This allows us to completely characterize the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$ on a Tamari lattice.\nL39: ## 1. Introduction\nL40: ### 1.1. Ornamentation Lattices\nL41: Laplante-Anfossi [cite20†15 ] recently introduced remarkable polytopes called operahedra. These polytopes are special examples of graph associahedra [cite21†5 , cite22†17 ] and poset associahedra [cite23†13 ], and they generalize both the associahedra and permutohedra. Laplante-Anfossi also considered certain posets obtained by orienting the $1$-skeletons of operahedra, and he asked if these posets are lattices.\nL42: Defant and Sack [cite24†11 ] answered Laplante-Anfossi’s question in the affirmative, naming his posets operahedron lattices. A key idea that Defant and Sack employed was to embed an operahedron lattice into the product of an interval in the weak order on the symmetric group and another lattice. This other lattice, which is the primary focus of this article, is a new structure called an ornamentation lattice.\nL43: Let $\\mathrm{PT}_{n}$ denote the set of rooted plane trees with $n$ nodes. Let $\\mathsf{T}\\in\\mathrm{PT}_{n}$. We view $\\mathsf{T}$ both as a graph and as a poset in which every non-root element is covered by exactly $1$ element (so the root is the unique maximal element). We denote the partial order on $\\mathsf{T}$ by $\\leq_{\\mathsf{T}}$. For $v\\in\\mathsf{T}$, we write\nL44:  | $$\\Delta_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)=\\{v^{\\prime}\\in\\mathsf{T}:v^{\\prime}\\leq_{\\mathsf{T}}v\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\nabla_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)=\\{v^{\\prime}\\in\\mathsf{T}:v\\leq_{\\mathsf{T}}v^{\\prime}\\}.$$  |\nL45: An ornament of $\\mathsf{T}$ is a set of nodes that induces a connected subgraph of $\\mathsf{T}$. Let $\\mathrm{Orn}(\\mathsf{T})$ denote the set of ornaments of $\\mathsf{T}$. We will often view an ornament as a rooted plane tree. Two sets are said to be nested if one is a subset of the other. An ornamentation of $\\mathsf{T}$ is a function $\\delta\\colon\\mathsf{T}\\to\\mathrm{Orn}(\\mathsf{T})$ such that\nL46: \nL47:   * •\nL48: \nL49: for every $v\\in\\mathsf{T}$, the unique maximal element of $\\delta(v)$ is $v$;\nL50: \nL51:   * •\nL52: for all $v,v^{\\prime}\\in\\mathsf{T}$, the ornaments $\\delta(v)$ and $\\delta(v^{\\prime})$ are either nested or disjoint.\nL53: \nL54: Let $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$ denote the set of ornamentations of $\\mathsf{T}$. We view $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$ as a poset, where the partial order $\\leq$ is defined so that $\\delta\\leq\\delta^{\\prime}$ if and only if $\\delta(v)\\subseteq\\delta^{\\prime}(v)$ for all $v\\in\\mathsf{T}$. Then $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$ is a lattice whose meet operation $\\wedge$ is such that\nL55:  | $$(\\delta\\wedge\\delta^{\\prime})(v)=\\delta(v)\\cap\\delta^{\\prime}(v)$$  |\nL56: \nL57: for all $v\\in\\mathsf{T}$. The minimum element $\\delta_{\\min}$ and maximum element $\\delta_{\\max}$ of $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$ satisfy\nL58: \nL59:  | $$\\delta_{\\min}(v)=\\{v\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\delta_{\\max}(v)=\\Delta_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)$$  |\nL60: \nL61: for all $v\\in\\mathsf{T}$. See cite25†Figure 1 . If $\\delta\\in\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$ and $v\\in\\mathsf{T}$, then we call $\\delta(v)$ an ornament of $\\delta$.\nL62: Ornamentation lattices naturally generalize Tamari lattices. Indeed, the $n$-th Tamari lattice is isomorphic to the ornamentation lattice of an $n$-element chain. Tamari lattices have numerous interesting properties; we will generalize some of these properties to the much broader family of ornamentation lattices.\nL63: \nL64: Figure 1. The ornamentation lattice of a rooted tree with $4$ nodes.\nL65: ### 1.2. Pop-Stack Operators\nL66: \nL67: The pop-stack operator of a finite lattice $L$ is the map $\\mathsf{Pop}\\colon L\\to L$ defined by\nL68: \nL69:  | $$\\mathsf{Pop}(x)=\\bigwedge(\\{x\\}\\cup\\{y\\in L:y\\lessdot x\\}).$$  |\nL70: Initial works on this operator focused on the case where $L$ is the weak order on the symmetric group [cite26†1 , cite27†2 , cite28†7 , cite29†8 , cite30†16 , cite31†18 , cite32†20 ], where it can be interpreted as a deterministic sorting procedure that makes use of a data structure called a pop-stack. Several recent articles have studied pop-stack operators on other interesting lattices, especially from a dynamical point of view [cite33†4 , cite34†6 , cite35†9 , cite36†10 , cite37†12 , cite38†14 ].\nL71: For example, Defant [cite36†10 ] and Hong [cite38†14 ] found that the pop-stack operator on a Tamari lattice has particularly well behaved dynamics. Choi and Sun [cite34†6 ] and Barnard, Defant, and Hanson [cite33†4 ] later generalized these results to the much broader class of Cambrian lattices. In this article, we generalize the results about Tamari lattices in a different direction by considering pop-stack operators of ornamentation lattices.\nL72: Given a finite lattice $L$ and an element $x\\in L$, we let $\\text{$\\mathchar 525$}_{\\mathsf{Pop}}(x)=\\{x,\\mathsf{Pop}(x),\\mathsf{Pop}^{2}(x),\\ldots\\}$ denote the forward orbit of $x$ under $\\mathsf{Pop}$. If $t$ is a sufficiently large integer, then $\\mathsf{Pop}^{t}(x)$ is the minimum element $\\hat{0}$ of $L$. We are interested in $|\\text{$\\mathchar 525$}_{\\mathsf{Pop}}(x)|$. When considering the pop-stack operator, one of the most well studied quantities associated to $L$ is\nL73:  | $$\\max_{x\\in L}|\\text{$\\mathchar 525$}_{\\mathsf{Pop}}(x)|$$  |\nL74: (see, e.g., [cite33†4 , cite35†9 , cite36†10 , cite32†20 ]). For example, Barnard, Defant, and Hanson found that if $L$ is a Cambrian lattice of a finite Coxeter group $W$, then $\\max_{x\\in L}|\\text{$\\mathchar 525$}_{\\mathsf{Pop}}(x)|$ is equal to the Coxeter number of $W$. In particular, if $L$ is the $n$-th Tamari lattice (which is a Cambrian lattice of the symmetric group $S_{n}$), then this number is $n$; this special case was proved earlier in [cite36†10 ].\nL75: Another important aspect of the pop-stack operator is its image, which has been studied for a variety of lattices in [cite33†4 , cite34†6 , cite37†12 , cite38†14 , cite39†19 ]. Defant and Williams found numerous different interpretations of the size of the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ when $L$ is a semidistributive lattice (or, more generally, a semidistrim lattice).\nL76: Notably, when $L$ is semidistributive, the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ is in bijection with the facets of a simplicial complex called the canonical join complex of $L$ (see [cite40†3 , cite33†4 ]). Hong [cite38†14 ] characterized and enumerated $\\mathsf{Pop}$ images on Tamari lattices. Choi and Sun [cite34†6 ] subsequently obtained analogous results for a variety of other lattices.\nL77: Barnard, Defant, and Hanson then unified several of these results by providing a simple Coxeter-theoretic description of the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ on an arbitrary Cambrian lattice of a finite Coxeter group. We aim to generalize Hong’s results concerning Tamari lattices in a different direction by studying $\\mathsf{Pop}$ images on ornamentation lattices.\nL78: ### 1.3. Main Results\nL79: \nL80: Let $\\mathsf{T}\\in\\mathrm{PT}_{n}$. Unless otherwise stated, we will write $\\mathsf{Pop}$ for the pop-stack operator on the ornamentation lattice $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$. Define the depth of a node $v\\in\\mathsf{T}$ to be the quantity $0pt_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)=|\\nabla_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)|-1$. Let $\\mathcal{M}_{\\mathsf{T}}$ denote the set of maximal chains in $\\mathsf{T}$.\nL81: ###### Theorem 1.1.\nL82: \nL83: Let $\\mathsf{T}\\in\\mathrm{PT}_{n}$, and let $\\mathfrak{r}$ be the root of $\\mathsf{T}$. Then\nL84: \nL85:  | $$\\max_{\\delta\\in\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})}|\\text{$\\mathchar 525$}_{\\mathsf{Pop}}(\\delta)|=\\max_{C\\in\\mathcal{M}_{\\mathsf{T}}}\\min_{v\\in C\\setminus\\{{\\mathfrak{r}}\\}}(|\\Delta_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)|+2\\,0pt_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)-1).$$  |\nL86: Let $\\mathsf{C}_{n}$ denote the chain with $n$ nodes. The ornamentation lattice $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{C}_{n})$ is the $n$-th Tamari lattice. In this special case, cite41†Theorem 1.1 tells us that the maximum size of a forward orbit of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ on the $n$-th Tamari lattice is $n$, which agrees with the aforementioned result from [cite36†10 ].\nL87: \nL88: The next theorem characterizes the image of the pop-stack operator on an ornamentation lattice, thereby generalizing a result of Hong from [cite38†14 ].\n"
